{\fldrslt{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf1 \u8220 On The Marionette Theater\u8221 }}}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  (1810) \u8212  \u8220 a wonderful, extraordinary engagement with the difference between innocence and experience,\u8221  Pullman said \u8212  and Marshall Berman\u8217 s 1982 book about modernism, \u8220 All that is Solid Melts into Air,\u8221  whose title comes from a line in Marx and Engels\u8217 s \u8220 Communist Manifesto.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb240\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Raised in Wales, Pullman worked as a teacher before becoming a full-time writer and has written more than three dozen books, for adults as well as children. In 2017, he published }{\field{\*\fldinst{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24
\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  HYPERLINK }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  }{
\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 https://www.nytimes.com/2018/10/22/books/review/philip-pullman-deamon-voices.html}{\*\datafield }}
{\fldrslt{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf1 \u8220 Daemon Voices: On Stories and Storytelling,\u8221 }}}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  a collection of lectures and essays about his influences and his craft. He has won numerous awards and been the subject of countless academic studies; his books have been turned into films, plays and a TV series. In 2019, he received a knighthood for \u8220 services to literature.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 I\u8217 m most grateful of all to those who continue to read my books, and I hope they don\u8217 t have to work as hard as those who edit them,\u8221  he said at the time.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}
\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 Now at work on a memoir, Pullman says he feels satisfied now that the story of Lyra has come to an end. \u8220 Lyra\u8217 s found the answer to the mystery she was set off with in this book: \u8216 What is my imagination? Where has it gone? Why has it vanished?\u8217 \u8221  he said. \u8220 And she\u8217 s discovered that the imagination is a faculty of seeing, not of imagining,\u8221  that takes in \u8220 all the memories, resemblances, metaphors \u8212  all the things that are connected with the things we see.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Of all his creations, Pullman is most proud of his daemons, who reflect the characters\u8217  understanding of themselves and whose narrative possibilities have expanded more creatively with each book. Because he has always said that no one can choose his own daemon (it\u8217 s up to our friends to identify the animal that best reflects us), it seems almost impertinent to broach the question: What sort of animal is his daemon?}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 As it happens, you can find an image of her by the artist John Lawrence on the cloth cover of \u8220 The Rose Field,\u8221  in an impression known as a blind stamp, along with the author\u8217 s signature. She\u8217 s a raven, Pullman said, someone who collects stories and is \u8220 a wise old bird who doesn\u8217 t talk very much, but when she does, she talks sense.\u8221 }
